PATCH 550: four more declarations narrowed to the width the image actually uses.

Found by `tools/width_triage.py` (board:0023) and each confirmed against the instruction.

## DGROUP:0x978c -- and the victory counter it was overwriting

    ab0a: a3 8c 97          mov   %ax,0x978c          ; `a3` = mov %ax,moffs16 -- TWO bytes
    afbb: f6 06 8c 97 03    testb $0x3,0x978c
    afd6: a0 8c 97          mov   0x978c,%al
   1abbb: a0 8c 97          mov   0x978c,%al

Four references, all byte or word, none 32-bit.  The port declared BOTH accessors too wide:
`_DAT_2000_578c` as `undefined4` and `DAT_2000_578c` as `undefined2`.  The dword one carries the only
store in the tree --

    _DAT_2000_578c = FUN_0000_0291();

-- so every call wrote 0x978c..0x978f and clobbered **DGROUP:0x978e, the live-object count that
FUN_1000_a5dc's victory test reads** (0x1a624 zeroes it, 0x1a636 counts it up, 0x1a694 tests it against
zero).  `FUN_0000_0291` returns `undefined4`; the asm keeps only AX, which the narrowing now does too.

This does NOT by itself explain why the victory outcome never fires -- a5dc zeroes, counts and tests
0x978e inside one call with no engine code in between, so the clobber cannot corrupt its own decision.
It does corrupt the leftover value 0x1a618 copies into `prev` (0x9792), which gates the count-decreased
notification at 0x1a66b, and it invalidated every `live=` figure measured from outside a5dc.

## DGROUP:0x9f1e -- a flag word updated by byte and by word, never as a dword

    bde4: 80 0e 1e 9f 20    orb   $0x20,0x9f1e
    bdeb: f6 06 1e 9f 10    testb $0x10,0x9f1e
    be37: 81 0e 1e 9f 20 03 orw   $0x320,0x9f1e

Same shape: `DAT_2000_5f1e` was `undefined2` where the asm reads and writes one byte, and
`_DAT_2000_5f1e` was `undefined4` where `orw` writes two, so `_DAT_2000_5f1e |= 800` (Ghidra's decimal
for 0x320) also wrote 0x9f20/0x9f21.

## DGROUP:0x9fd6 -- a byte sitting directly below a live word

    beb7: 80 3e d6 9f ff    cmpb  $0xff,0x9fd6
    bebc: 74 1d             je    0xbedb
    bebe: a1 d7 9f          mov   0x9fd7,%ax          ; DAT_2000_5fd7 -- a WORD
    bec1: 3b 06 de 6c       cmp   0x6cde,%ax
    bec7: a0 d6 9f          mov   0x9fd6,%al
    bed6: c6 06 d6 9f ff    movb  $0xff,0x9fd6
    bedc: c6 06 d6 9f ff    movb  $0xff,0x9fd6
    beef: a2 d6 9f          mov   %al,0x9fd6

Five references, every one byte-width.  Declared `undefined2`, so `DAT_2000_5fd6 = 0xff` wrote 0x00ff
and zeroed 0x9fd7 -- the word the guard on the very next line reads.  `DAT_2000_5fd7` is correctly
declared `undefined2` and is written by `mov %bx,0x9fd7` at 0xbef6, so the neighbour is real state.

Narrowing it forces its two sentinel constants: the asm compares and stores the BYTE 0xff, and an
unsigned char promotes to int, so the Ghidra-emitted `-1` would be a constant false.  Those two lines
change here rather than in patch 551 because the narrowing is what requires them.

